browse on OS-9 -- what was changed==================================Peter da Silva's screen-oriented directory browser, ported to OS-9/68000 byCarsten Emde (Klinikum Steglitz, Freie Universitaet Berlin) and posted tocomp.os.os9 in January 1990.  It shows an `ls -l' listing you move around inthe way you would move around a file in `vi', and acts on the entry under thecursor: inspect it, dump it in hex, rename, copy, delete, edit or run it.ORIG/ holds the posting as it came.  Read that directory before this file:the release arrived in two goes, and the second corrects the first.  browse_2a, browse_2b   the source as it finally stood.  Emde reposted it on                         3 February 1990 -- "Sorry for problems with this                         source when posted some weeks ago.  `browse.c' comes                         now in two parts" -- and the shar's own instructions                         say to merge the two.  That merge is SRC/browse's                         browse.c, and it is what this collection builds.  browse_jan.c           the 6 January posting, the one he apologised for.                         Kept because ORIG/ keeps a release as it was sent.  browse.hlp             the help text, shipped here as SYS/browse.hlp.  makefile               his own, naming os9lib.l and termlib.l.ONE CHANGE, and it is the pager.    #define ASSISTCOM "more /dd/sys/browse.hlp"      as posted    #define ASSISTCOM "less /dd/sys/browse.hlp"      hereThe `?' key pipes the help file through whatever that names, and when thiswas changed there was no `more' on this disk -- the pager was `less', whichreads TERM and TERMCAP the same way.  With `more' the key did nothing atall.  (A `more' has arrived since; `less' still pages the help better.)The change is inside the existing `#ifdef OSK', so ORIG still builds whereit came from.AND THE HELP FILE, SYS/browse.hlp, differs from ORIG/browse.hlp in twolines.  The port moved the line keys to i and , (with . and o for twentylines) and updated the help for three of them but not the fourth: it said`k,^K  Go up a line', and k does nothing here; i does.  It now says i.  And`?' is said to page the help through `less', which is what it does.Corrected 2026-09-27.AND THE YEAR.  The listing printed tm_year, years since 1900, with %02d,so 2026 read `126'.  It prints the year modulo 100, as it did when it waswritten.  One line of browse.c (the original is browse.c.ori), rebuilt-qm, 2026-09-27.WHAT WAS NOT CHANGED, and why two things that look like faults are not:  /h0/sys/browse.macros   the default macro file.  It is only the default:                          `BROWSE' in the environment replaces it, and the                          save prompt offers it as an editable suggestion.  /bin/sh, /bin/pwd       both are inside the `#else' arm, the one compiled                          when OSK is not defined.  Under OS-9 `sys()' does                          not fork them at all.  /h0/CMDS/pd             a string in the built module that is in no line of                          browse.c -- it arrives with the library code linked                          in.  DOC/DEPENDS lists it with `--' because it is                          not on this disk, and browse never opens it.  See                          the note at the head of DEPENDS about fallback                          paths that a program carries and never uses.WHAT IT NEEDS TO RUN.  TERM and TERMCAP, which SYS/login sets.  And for thekeys that run a program -- `?', `$', `&', and the editor and copy macros --a shell: this C library's system() forks the bare module name `shell', sothose keys do nothing unless one is resident.  SYS/login loads yours from/h1 if you have an OS-9 system mounted there; DOC/README-SHELLS explains it.